      dek: 'The deal with Katoen Natie extended the container terminal by 50 years. The Court of Accounts backed it 4 to 3 and criticised the port authority\'s chairman.',
      contexto: 'Terminal Cuenca del Plata (TCP) handles containers at the port of Montevideo; its capital is 80% Belgian group Katoen Natie and 20% state-owned, through the National Port Administration (ANP). Under Luis Lacalle Pou\'s government the State agreed to extend the concession by 50 more years, to 2081, in exchange for quay investments and with priority conditions for TCP in container-ship operations; la diaria dates the signing of the agreement to April 2021. On 15 September 2021 the ANP board approved it and sent it to the Court of Accounts, which ruled the following year.',
      hallazgo: 'La diaria reported that the ANP\'s Frente Amplio board member, Alejandra Koch, said there had "not been a single argument" on the board in favour of the new agreement and that she hoped the Court of Accounts would object to it, while the body\'s chairman, Juan Curbelo, argued the committed investments would make "the big ships choose" the Montevideo terminal. On 30 March 2022 the Court of Accounts ruled by a majority — four votes to three, according to la diaria — that the Executive\'s conduct was "in accordance with law" and that "there are no objections from a legal standpoint", adding that "it is not apparent that the new management regime contravenes the Ports law or the Competition Defence law". According to El Observador, that same opinion objected that the advisory opinions required by law were arranged by the board chairman rather than issued "by the full board", and that the advice on the berthing regulations came after the administrative decision. In May 2024 Koch told UyPress that "the numbers they put forward are not quite so": of a total investment of US$ 330 million the ANP would contribute US$ 134 million, so Katoen Natie\'s contribution would be US$ 196 million, not the US$ 600 million announced by the government.',
